   "properties": {},
   "widgets_values": [
    "## Bake & output\nProjects all views onto the UVs and saves to `ComfyUI/output/UltraTex/`:\n- a textured **GLB** (also shown in Preview 3D)\n- the **albedo** and **ORM** PNGs\n\n`texture_size` is the final texture resolution (2048 is a good default). It is independent of the sampler resolution.\n\n### 10-view bake\n- `views_2_use = no top/bottom`: the second rig's top and bottom cameras sit exactly where pass 1's are, so only its 4 side views are baked. **6 + 4 = 10 views.**\n- Albedo uses all 10 views. Metallic-roughness uses the 6 views of pass 1, which is plenty for low-detail maps and saves a whole pass."
   ],
   "title": "Read me \u00b7 Bake",
   "color": "#432",
   "bgcolor": "#653"
  },
  {
   "id": 29,
   "type": "MarkdownNote",
   "pos": [
    1540,
    1860
   ],
   "size": [
    800,
    640
   ],
   "flags": {},
   "order": 29,
   "mode": 0,
   "inputs": [],
   "outputs": [],
   "properties": {},
   "widgets_values": [
    "## 6 \u00b7 Pass 2: 4 extra views at 45\u00b0\nWith 6 cameras, anything **between** them (sides of the arms, a holster on the hip, the shoulders) is only seen at a steep angle, and that's where smears come from.\n\n**How it works**\n1. **Rotate Rig** turns the 6 cameras by 45\u00b0. That gives 4 new side angles at 45 / 135 / 225 / 315\u00b0.\n2. **Render Views** projects the pass 1 result onto those new cameras and marks what pass 1 already saw head-on (`keep_above 0.75`).\n3. **Pass 2** starts from that image at **denoise 0.6**, so only ~11 of 25 steps run. It keeps what's already good and only refines the rest, so both passes agree.\n\n**Cost:** about **+45%** sampling time.\n**Gain** (developer tests): the share of the surface seen head-on went from 44 \u2192 58% (dwarf) and 60 \u2192 75% (anime character).\n\n\u26a0\ufe0f **Never** connect pass 1's `views` directly to pass 2. They come from the other camera rig and must go through **Render Views**.\n\n**Keep `resolution` the same** on pass 1, Render Views and pass 2 (1024 or 2048)."
   ],
   "title": "Read me \u00b7 10 views",
   "color": "#323",
   "bgcolor": "#545"
  },
  {
   "id": 30,
   "type": "MarkdownNote",
   "pos": [
    0,
    -760
   ],
   "size": [
    1460,
    720
   ],
   "flags": {},
   "order": 30,
   "mode": 0,
   "inputs": [],
   "outputs": [],
   "properties": {},
   "widgets_values": [
    "# UltraTex \u00b7 Advanced (10 views + PBR)\n**PixelArtistry** \u00b7 nodes by **visualbruno** (ComfyUI-UltraTex, MIT) \u00b7 model by the **UltraTex** team (SIGGRAPH Asia 2026)\n\nSame as the standard workflow, plus a **second pass with 4 cameras at 45\u00b0**. Use it for final characters with a lot going on at the sides: arms, belts, holsters, shoulder pads. For quick tests and simple shapes, the 6-view workflow is enough.\n\n### How to use\n1. **Group 1:** set your mesh path and load your reference image.\n2. Press **Run**.\n3. Open the GLB in **Preview 3D**, or grab the files from `output/UltraTex/`.\n\n### Settings that matter\n| Setting | Where | Value |\n|---|---|---|\n| Resolution | Pass 1, Render Views, Pass 2 | the same value: 1024 tests, 2048 finals |\n| Pass 2 strength | Pass 2 \u00b7 denoise | 0.6 (lower = keeps more of pass 1) |\n| Keep threshold | Render Views \u00b7 keep_above | 0.75 \u2248 seen within 41\u00b0 of head-on |\n| Texture size | Bake \u00b7 texture_size | 2048 |\n| VRAM | \u2013 | same as standard (~9 GB fp8 model, tested on 16 GB). For 6\u20138 GB use the GGUF workflow |\n\n### Models (the installer puts them in place)\n| File | Folder |\n|---|---|\n| `flux-2-klein-base-9b-fp8.safetensors` | `models/diffusion_models` |\n| `qwen_3_8b_fp8mixed.safetensors` | `models/text_encoders` |\n| `flux2-vae.safetensors` | `models/vae` |\n| `ultratex_flux2.safetensors` | `models/loras` |\n| `ultratex_flux2_mr.safetensors` | `models/loras` |\n| `ultratex_flux2_decoder.pt` | `models/ultratex` |\n| `birefnet.safetensors` | `models/background_removal` |\n\n**Must be Klein BASE 9B.** The normal (distilled) Klein 9B does not work with UltraTex.\n\n### License\n- UltraTex code and nodes: MIT.\n- FLUX.2 Klein base 9B: FLUX Non-Commercial License v2.1. BFL claims no ownership of your outputs and allows using them for any purpose, including commercial, except training a competing model. Running the model itself is licensed for non-commercial use, so check https://bfl.ai/licensing before client or production work.\n"
   ],
   "title": "START HERE \u00b7 UltraTex 10 views",
   "color": "#223",
   "bgcolor": "#335"
  }
